practice.answer:
Egan notes that research has studied therapists less than treatments, yet shows the person of the therapist matters more than the method and that some therapists are better than others. Adapting Wampold's 2011 review, an effective therapist:

- has solid interpersonal skills expressing acceptance, warmth and empathy, and builds trust;
- forms a collaborative working alliance and agrees goals with the client;
- understands the client's condition and offers a plausible explanation of the distress, in its cultural, social, economic and political context;
- has a flexible treatment approach and explains it clearly;
- is persuasive without robbing the client of autonomy or dignity;
- monitors progress collaboratively, sets up feedback and adjusts the process accordingly;
- fosters realistic hope and optimism;
- tackles difficult issues tactfully;
- understands self and self-discloses only when helpful;
- knows relevant research and is committed to self-improvement;
- can tailor the key ingredients of therapy to client outcomes.

Egan personalises this through Laura, who adapts to the client rather than her theories, is a catalyst rather than an advice-giver, admits mistakes and uses two-way feedback. He adds that preferences differ (lower-SES clients wanted advice and reassurance), so ingredients should be tailored without indulging clients.
